<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
    <dict>
        <!-- Deliberately minimal. Two keys, both of which are stable for the life of the product.

             What is NOT here, and why:

             `NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes` is optional, and listing the 15 types published under App
             Privacy in App Store Connect would duplicate a declaration that already exists and is
             already authoritative — the label is what users see and what review reads; nothing
             validates this file against it. Two copies with no mechanism keeping them in step is a
             drift generator, and a manifest that has fallen behind the label is worse than an absent
             one, because it is a false statement rather than a missing one. Add a type here only if
             Apple ever starts enforcing agreement.

             `NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes` is required only for required-reason APIs called by *this
             target*. `AppDelegate.swift` and `NextExportViewController.swift` between them touch
             `FileManager.fileExists` and `contentsOfDirectory`, neither of which is on Apple's list;
             everything else is pod code, and each pod ships its own manifest that Xcode aggregates.
             An earlier version of this file declared `UserDefaults` on the grounds that Capacitor
             uses it — true, but that is Capacitor's declaration to make, not ours. If an upload ever
             returns ITMS-91053, add the category the warning names and nothing more. -->

        <!-- The app does not track. Tracking, in Apple's sense, is linking data collected here with
             third-party data for advertising, or handing it to a data broker; Compass does neither.
             Nothing reads the IDFA, and PostHog — the one third-party analytics tool the web build
             uses — is switched off entirely in the iOS shell (`posthogEnabled()` in
             web/lib/service/analytics.ts), along with the consent banner App Review read as a custom
             tracking prompt in 1.42.0 (11).

             ⚠️ This key states what *our* code does; it does not override a linked SDK. Xcode builds
             the app's privacy report by aggregating every manifest in the binary, so an SDK that
             declares tracking makes the app declare tracking no matter what this file says. See
             `docs/design/sign-in-with-apple/` — as of this writing FBSDKCoreKit is linked via
             @capgo/capacitor-social-login and declares exactly that. -->
        <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
        <false/>
        <!-- Empty by definition: a domain listed here is one the app contacts for tracking, and there
             are none of ours. Not omitted — an explicit empty array is the unambiguous statement. -->
        <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
        <array/>
    </dict>
</plist>
